WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.170
Film Courage: Gary, what’s the greatest
script you’ve ever read and what happened

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.839
to that script?

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:20.550
Gary W. Goldstein: Wow...You know, I’ve
read a lot of what I would consider...I’ve

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:31.939
been blessed to read a lot of great scripts.
And some of them never got made, fortunately

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:40.900
many have. I remember reading the MILLION
DOLLAR HOTEL by a friend of mine (Nicholas

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:49.470
Klein) and I was so endlessly in love with
that script. And I wanted him to direct (he

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:58.510
had never directed). It ended up being directed
by Wim Wenders and (in my estimation) it didn’t

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:05.960
have the poetry that was Nicholas that was
infused in it because of who he was. No disrespect

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:14.820
to Wim Wenders. He’s a lauded filmmaker
of course. But in that case, it kind of went

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.850
one direction.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:24.410
PRETTY WOMAN before it was PRETTY WOMAN (3000)
by J. F. Lawton was certainly at that time

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:29.890
the best script I’d ever held in my hands.
And it was a dark drama of course but it was

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:39.350
absolutely riveting, compelling, in a sort
of tragic sense. And of course as we know

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:46.130
that film did get made, only it got...ironically
the film got made so true to its original

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:52.550
form, but just the structure and the characters
and the dialogue was so taught that we didn’t

00:01:52.550 --> 00:02:00.799
have to labor to turn it tonally. And of course,
we had Garry Marshall, so he could improv anything.

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:06.590
He filmed it as it was and then he did a little
improv and we found that sort of comedy in

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:07.700
it.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.450
So that got made, again...in a totally different
direction. Originally it was going to be a

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:17.780
dark independent, few-million-dollar-film.
So you never know? These things always take

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.080
left turns.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.569
But there have been a lot of great scripts
that sadly didn’t get made. I remember there

00:02:26.569 --> 00:02:35.310
was a little film called THE CURE that I was
madly in love with. A very special small script,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:41.849
young characters. And I had actually shared
that with Steven Spielberg and his people

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:46.350
flipped out. And then Steven fell in love
with it and we were cranking up the offer

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:56.319
from Universal and Warner Brothers simultaneously.
The agent actually sold the script overnight

00:02:56.319 --> 00:03:04.670
to a third party and it got made years later
as a low-budget, kind of non-event. That was

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:07.520
the biggest heartbreak personally for me.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:15.640
But you never know. There are scripts today
that probably still haven’t been made that

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:22.940
will get made that I think are amazing. There’s
no predicting this business.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:28.360
Film Courage: So what was it about that script
where you said was eventually sold to a third-party?

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:34.570
I mean what was it that just really resonated
with you? Was it a personal thing that you

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:39.480
felt a connection to? Or the way it was written,
the voice of the protagonist?

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:49.129
Gary W. Goldstein: What was so remarkable
about it was there are characters and stories

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:59.370
that are so gorgeous because of their truth,
because they call to us, they penetrate us

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:05.120
in a way that is undeniable. That all of us
have had that experience growing up. And this

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:10.930
[script] was about growing up...young characters.
And anyone who has blood flowing through their

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:17.340
veins would have recognized some part of themselves
or their friends in that story in a very poignant

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:25.259
context. Because it was so universal and so
sweetly done, it wasn’t a sweet picture,

00:04:25.259 --> 00:04:30.551
it was a challenging story. But what was sweet
about it was the attention to detail, the

00:04:30.551 --> 00:04:33.680
truth of how it played out.

